trajectory.patch:
The root cause of the test failure is an inconsistency in the devicetree configuration for the `native_64` and `native_sim` boards used in this test. The test logic derives the flash size from the `flash0` devicetree node, which is set to 1MB by the overlay. However, the flash simulator driver for the POSIX architecture gets its size from its parent node, `flashcontroller0`, which remains at its default size of 2MB from the base board DTS.

This discrepancy causes the out-of-bounds check to fail. The test attempts to access memory just beyond the 1MB boundary, which it expects to be invalid. The driver, configured with a 2MB memory region, considers this access valid and does not return the expected `-EINVAL` error, leading to the assertion failure.

The fix is to update the board overlays to set the size of `flashcontroller0` to be consistent with `flash0`, ensuring both the test and the driver operate on a flash memory of the same size.

tests/drivers/flash_simulator/flash_sim_impl/boards/native_ev_0x00.overlay
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
&flashcontroller0 {
	erase-value = <0x00>;
};
========
&flashcontroller0 {
	erase-value = <0x00>;
	reg = <0x00000000 DT_SIZE_K(1024)>;
};
>>>>>>>> REPLACE
tests/drivers/flash_simulator/flash_sim_impl/boards/native_sim.overlay
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
&flash0 {
	erase-block-size = <1024>;
	write-block-size = <4>;
	reg = <0x00000000 DT_SIZE_K(1024)>;
};
========
&flashcontroller0 {
	reg = <0x00000000 DT_SIZE_K(1024)>;
};

&flash0 {
	erase-block-size = <1024>;
	write-block-size = <4>;
	reg = <0x00000000 DT_SIZE_K(1024)>;
};
